3. Numerical methods from the literature
----------------------------------------
The special functions behind the chi-square, t and F tail probabilities
follow published algorithm descriptions:

  * the regularized upper incomplete gamma function Q(a, x)
    (native/spatex_core/src/esda/losh.rs, dynamics/special.rs and
    regression/dist.rs) uses the power series for x < a + 1 and the
    continued fraction evaluated with the modified Lentz method otherwise,
    as described in Press, W. H. et al., Numerical Recipes, section 6.2;
  * the regularized incomplete beta function (beta_cf and betai in
    regression/dist.rs) uses the continued fraction with the modified Lentz
    method and the symmetry I_x(a, b) = 1 - I_(1-x)(b, a), as described in
    section 6.4 of the same book;
  * ln Gamma is the Lanczos approximation with g = 7 and nine coefficients in
    esda/losh.rs and dynamics/special.rs, and libm's lgamma in
    regression/dist.rs.


4. Test fixtures and generators
-------------------------------
test/reference/fixtures holds JSON produced at development time by running
the upstream packages through the generators in test/reference/*.py. The
fixtures contain:

  * input data copied from datasets distributed with libpysal
    (libpysal.examples), among them Columbus, sids2, mexico, Georgia
    (GData_utm) and NAT (NCOVR), alongside synthetic data; and
  * numerical output of libpysal, esda, mapclassify, pointpats, spreg, mgwr,
    giddy, inequality, spopt, fast_hdbscan, NumPy, SciPy and shapely.

The generators call those packages; a few restate short upstream expressions
(for example spreg's moment and likelihood objectives) to evaluate them at
given parameters. The example datasets are distributed with libpysal, whose
license is in LICENSES/libpysal.txt. Neither the fixtures nor the generators
are part of the Hex package.


5. Notebook datasets
--------------------
The datasets under notebooks/data are copies of datasets distributed with
libpysal (BSD 3-Clause License, Copyright (c) 2007-, PySAL Developers; see
LICENSES/libpysal.txt). Each folder's README.md describes its contents.

North Carolina SIDS data
    notebooks/data/nc_sids holds the sids2 dataset as distributed with
    libpysal and the GeoDa Center. Source: Cressie, N. (1993), Statistics for
    Spatial Data, Wiley, pp. 386-389.

US state income data
    notebooks/data/us_income holds the us_income dataset as distributed with
    libpysal. Source: Regional Economic Information System, Bureau of
    Economic Analysis, U.S. Department of Commerce.

Baltimore house sales data
    notebooks/data/baltimore holds the baltim dataset as distributed with
    libpysal. Source: Dubin, R. A. (1992), Regional Science and Urban
    Economics 22(3), 433-452.

Georgia county data
    notebooks/data/georgia holds the georgia dataset as distributed with
    libpysal. Source: Fotheringham, A. S., Brunsdon, C. and Charlton, M.
    (2002), Geographically Weighted Regression, Wiley.

John Snow cholera map data
    notebooks/data/snow holds the snow_maps dataset as distributed with
    libpysal, a digitization of the map in Snow, J. (1855), On the Mode of
    Communication of Cholera.

Mexican state income data
    notebooks/data/mexico holds the mexico dataset as distributed with
    libpysal. Source: Rey, S. J. and Sastre Gutierrez, M. L. (2010), Spatial
    Economic Analysis 5, 277-298.
